AndroidiOSReact NativeFlutterCapacitorCordova <p>Call <code>enroll</code> on each frame you want to keep. Each call adds one sample under the same ID, up to 25.</p> <pre><code>if (result.faceState == FaceRecognitionState.Detected &amp;&amp; result.isConfident()) {
    val faceId = result.faceIDs.keys.first()
    YEOFRSDK.enroll(faceId, "user-id", learnFromCurrentFrame = true)   // 0 means success
}
</code></pre> <p>Space the calls a little apart, prompt small head turns while they run, and save when you are done with <code>faceRecognitionTrackerData()</code>.</p> <p>Each <code>enroll(name:)</code> adds one sample, up to 25, and saves the file for you.</p> <pre><code>for await step in service.enroll(name: "user-id") {
    // step.phase: .capturing (show step.prompt), then .completed or .failed
}
</code></pre> <p>Call it again as the user turns slightly or moves to other light. You can also enrol from photos you hold with <code>enroll(name:images:)</code>. For a full walk through five head positions from your own camera loop, see <code>PoseWalkEnrollment</code> in the API reference.</p> <p>Set <code>enrolName</code>, wait for <code>registered</code>, then save.</p> <pre><code>&lt;YeofrCamera
  enrolName={enrolling ? 'user-id' : undefined}
  enrolPoses={['front', 'yawLeft', 'yawRight', 'pitchUp', 'pitchDown']}
  onEvent={(e) =&gt; handle(e.nativeEvent)}
/&gt;
</code></pre> <pre><code>if (event.type === 'registered') await saveTrackerToDisk();
</code></pre> <ul> <li>Android: the poses make a paced walk of at least five seconds.</li> <li>iOS: the capture is a single front capture, and <code>enrolProgress</code> reports one <code>front</code> pose.</li> <li>Cancelling: on iOS, clearing <code>enrolName</code> stops the events but not the capture, so a face can still be stored afterwards. To abandon a capture on either platform, reload the saved gallery or clear it.</li> </ul> <p>Your app runs the capture loop. Each <code>enroll</code> adds one sample, up to 25, so call it on every frame you want to keep.</p> <pre><code>if (result.faceState == FaceState.detected &amp;&amp; result.isLive) {
  final faceId = result.faceIDs.firstOrNull?.index;
  if (faceId != null) await YeoFR.enroll(faceId, 'user-id');   // 0 means success
}

// When the capture is finished:
final bytes = await YeoFR.saveTracker();
if (bytes != null) await saveTrackerToStorage(bytes);
</code></pre> <p>Space the calls a little apart and prompt small head turns while they run.</p> <pre><code>await Yeofr.enrol({
  name: customerId,
  poses: ['front', 'yawLeft', 'yawRight', 'pitchUp', 'pitchDown'],
});
</code></pre> <p>Show progress from <code>enrolProgress</code> events using <code>posesAccepted</code> and <code>posesPlanned</code>. The capture is finished when <code>registered</code> arrives.</p> <ul> <li>The poses are prompts. Neither platform measures head position, so they guide the user but do not block the capture.</li> <li>Android: each pose is prompted for 3 seconds before its frames count.</li> <li>iOS: there is one capture per pose, with a short pause before each. Sending the app to the background ends the capture, so start it again when the user returns.</li> <li>A capture replaces the enrolled identity. If it is cut short, the previous enrolment is put back.</li> </ul> <pre><code>await cordova.plugins.Yeofr.enrol({
  name: customerId,
  poses: ['front', 'yawLeft', 'yawRight', 'pitchUp', 'pitchDown'],
});
</code></pre> <p>Show progress from <code>enrolProgress</code> events using <code>posesAccepted</code> and <code>posesPlanned</code>. The capture is finished when <code>registered</code> arrives.</p> <ul> <li>The poses are prompts. Neither platform measures head position, so they guide the user but do not block the capture.</li> <li>Android: each pose is prompted for 3 seconds before its frames count.</li> <li>iOS: there is one capture per pose, with a short pause before each. Sending the app to the background ends the capture, so start it again when the user returns.</li> <li>A capture replaces the enrolled identity. If it is cut short, the previous enrolment is put back.</li> </ul>

AndroidiOSReact NativeFlutterCapacitorCordova <pre><code>val live = result.faceState == FaceRecognitionState.Detected &amp;&amp; result.isConfident()
val accepted = live &amp;&amp; result.faceIDs.values.contains(expectedUser)
</code></pre> <p><code>faceState == Detected</code> matters. With a second face in view, the last verdict is held for up to 1.5 seconds, so <code>isConfident()</code> alone can still be true.</p> <pre><code>for await update in service.updates() {
    let accepted = update.status == .trusted &amp;&amp; update.matchedName == expectedUser
}
</code></pre> <p><code>.trusted</code> combines liveness with the identity check. On phones with a TrueDepth camera, it also includes depth.</p> <p>To limit recognition to one person when several are enrolled, use <code>setActiveRecognitionIdentity(_:)</code>.</p> <pre><code>if (event.type === 'result') {
  const accepted = event.status === 'trusted' &amp;&amp; event.matchedName === expectedUser;
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>final accepted = result.isLive &amp;&amp; result.faceIDs.firstOrNull?.id == expectedUser;
</code></pre> <p><code>isLive</code> is already false when no face, or more than one face, is in view.</p> <pre><code>await Yeofr.addListener('result', (r) =&gt; {
  const accepted = r.status === 'trusted' &amp;&amp; r.matchedName === expectedUser;
});
</code></pre> <pre><code>await cordova.plugins.Yeofr.addListener('result', (r) =&gt; {
  const accepted = r.status === 'trusted' &amp;&amp; r.matchedName === expectedUser;
});
</code></pre>

AndroidiOSReact NativeFlutterCapacitorCordova <p>Encrypt the bytes with a key from the Android Keystore, for example AES-GCM, and store the result in your app's private storage.</p> <pre><code>val gallery: ByteArray? = YEOFRSDK.faceRecognitionTrackerData()
// encrypt with your Keystore key, then store

// On the next launch, after init:
val code = YEOFRSDK.loadTracker(decryptedBytes)   // negative means refused
</code></pre> <p>You can give the SDK your own <code>Cryptor</code>, for example one built on a CryptoKit authenticated cipher, and read and write the gallery through it.</p> <pre><code>let sdk = try YEOFRSDK(pilotUnlockCode: credentialFromYeo,
                       encryption: .custom(MyCryptor()))

// Save
if let blob = try sdk.encryptedFaceRecognitionTrackerData() {
    try blob.serialized().write(to: url, options: .atomic)
}

// Load
try sdk.loadTracker(from: EncryptedBlob.parse(Data(contentsOf: url)))
</code></pre> <p><code>FaceTrustService</code> still writes an unencrypted file</p> <p>Handing an encrypting SDK to <code>FaceTrustService(sdk:)</code> does not encrypt its saved file. For encrypted storage, your app must own the saving, and it must also remove the service's own file. <code>Examples/FRPilot</code> in the Swift package shows a worked example.</p> <p>Use <code>getTrackerData()</code> and <code>loadTracker()</code> instead of the disk helpers. Encrypt the base64 string with a key from the Keychain or Keystore, and store it yourself.</p> <pre><code>const data = await getTrackerData();           // encrypt, then store
const result = await loadTracker(decrypted);   // use isOK(result) to check
</code></pre> <p>Encrypt the bytes with a key from the Keychain or Keystore, and store them yourself.</p> <pre><code>final bytes = await YeoFR.saveTracker();   // encrypt, then store
await YeoFR.loadTracker(decrypted);        // negative means refused
</code></pre> <p>Read the template once <code>registered</code> has arrived, store it encrypted, and put it back before <code>start()</code>.</p> <pre><code>const data = await Yeofr.getTrackerData();
// encrypt, then store. Later:
await Yeofr.loadTracker(decrypted);
</code></pre> <p>The plugin's default store still keeps a copy until you call <code>clearTracker()</code>.</p> <p>Read the template once <code>registered</code> has arrived, store it encrypted, and put it back before <code>start()</code>.</p> <pre><code>const data = await cordova.plugins.Yeofr.getTrackerData();
// encrypt, then store. Later:
await cordova.plugins.Yeofr.loadTracker(decrypted);
</code></pre> <p>The plugin's default store still keeps a copy until you call <code>clearTracker()</code>.</p>
